WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:07.200
When I say the word “monster” you might think&nbsp;
of this or this or even this . We might use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.320
it to describe something huge or intimidating,&nbsp;
but what most of us don’t think about is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.520
the word came from. As far as our brains&nbsp;
are concerned, word origins are irrelevant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.600
All that matters is that you think of&nbsp;
the same thing I do when I speak it.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.360
So why do we bother searching for the origins of&nbsp;
words? To answer that question (and many more),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.480
I’m excited to introduce the newest&nbsp;
addition to the Storied channel: Otherwords,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:39.200
a show all about language, hosted by sociolinguist&nbsp;
and certified word nerd Dr. Erica Brozovsky.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.720
Thanks, Dr. Z! The study of the&nbsp;
origins of words, known as etymology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.960
goes way deeper than just opening a&nbsp;
dictionary. Just like living creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:55.840
words don’t pop up fully-formed. They evolve over&nbsp;
thousands of years, with ancestors that stretch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.440
back to the earliest Homo sapiens. Tracing the&nbsp;
history of a word shows us how human ideas change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.720
over time, and reveals connections between&nbsp;
cultures that would otherwise be forgotten.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.720
So let’s follow this monster’s&nbsp;
footprints into the prehistoric past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.160
across multiple continents and several languages,&nbsp;
to a place that only exists in theory...

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.640
“Monster” entered the English lexicon as a&nbsp;
version of the French monstre sometime between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.640
the 11th and 13th centuries, as a result&nbsp;
of the Norman Occupation of England. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.280
William the Conqueror invaded England and&nbsp;
installed a bunch of French nobles to rule it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.280
they brought a lot of French words with&nbsp;
them. This is one of the reasons why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.520
English, though technically a Germanic&nbsp;
language, is filled with Latin words.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.240
Because the invaders were the new aristocracy&nbsp;
in England, their fancy Latin words tended to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:58.240
be things related to government, academia, and&nbsp;
technology. To this day, English words for simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.240
or domestic things are usually Germanic in origin,&nbsp;
like house, food and bread, while words that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.760
more “intellectual” are usually from Latin, like&nbsp;
representative, dissertation, and combustion.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:16.160
This class distinction was so dramatic that&nbsp;
when peasants raised livestock on their farms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:22.800
they used the Anglo-Saxon words pig, sheep, and&nbsp;
cow. But when the meat was served to the nobles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.080
they switched to the French:&nbsp;
pork, mutton, and beef.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.240
But how did France get words from Rome?&nbsp;
You can thank Julius Caesar for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.400
Around 55 B.C., he invaded what is now&nbsp;
France, which at the time was occupied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.680
by a Celtic-speaking people known as the Gauls.&nbsp;
Over time, a colloquial version of Latin called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:47.600
Vulgar Latin mixed with the Gallic language&nbsp;
to create what we now know as French. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.120
all the romance languages, including Italian,&nbsp;
Spanish and Portuguese, evolved from Vulgar Latin.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:59.920
Like most French words, monstre has a Latin root:&nbsp;
monstrum, which meant evil omen. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.080
the appearance of strange creatures&nbsp;
or animals with birth defects was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.040
considered a warning that something bad was&nbsp;
about to happen. It may seem silly today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.720
but you can kind of see how, long before&nbsp;
anyone understood genetic mutation or prenatal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.520
development, having a two-headed goat pop up&nbsp;
on your farm might make you feel like someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.000
was trying to tell you something--and&nbsp;
that something probably wasn’t good.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.840
Monstrum was derived from the Latin verb monere,&nbsp;
which meant “to warn, remind or instruct.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:39.040
A lot of other English words are derived from&nbsp;
monere, like premonition, admonish, demonstrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:44.480
and monitor. When words share an original&nbsp;
root like this, we call them cognates.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.360
Cognate comes from the Latin for “born together,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.680
essentially implying that these words are&nbsp;
siblings. Incidentally, since they share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:58.080
a common parent, the words nation, nature&nbsp;
and pregnancy are all cognates of cognate.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.920
Some people think that the Roman goddess Juno&nbsp;
Moneta was also named after monere, because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:09.280
as the protectress of the city’s funds, she&nbsp;
“warned” against economic instability. Her name is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.960
where we get the words money and mint, so if true,&nbsp;
we can add them to the list of monster’s cognates.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:15.470
It seems like the word monster has changed a lot&nbsp;
since its origins in Ancient Rome, but maybe not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:15.600
as much as you’d think.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.200
Today, we don’t explicitly associate monsters with&nbsp;
bad omens, but it is there if you look for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.560
I mean, what is Godzilla but a warning about&nbsp;
the dangers of atomic power? Mary Shelley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:32.240
used Frankenstein’s monster as a warning about&nbsp;
scientific hubris, and even George Romero used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.478
zombies to warn us about social ills like racism,&nbsp;
commercialism, and inequality. Somehow, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.478 --> 00:04:37.760
notion of scary beasts being harbingers of worse&nbsp;
things to come is still somewhere in our psyche.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.480
That’s why I named my show Monstrum, because&nbsp;
when you spend your life studying monsters, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.240
start to see that underneath the teeth and claws&nbsp;
and fur are human fears about very real things.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.880
Okay, so we’ve established that the scary,&nbsp;
mythical beast we now call “monster,” was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:59.440
originally monere, a warning or a lesson. But is&nbsp;
that the end of the road? After all, monere didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:06.080
come out of nowhere. It, too, must have ancestors.&nbsp;
But to find them, we have to travel back before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:12.240
the written word, which means we don’t have much&nbsp;
hard evidence, and have to rely instead on theory.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.840
In the late 18th century, the British philologist&nbsp;
William Jones noticed striking similarities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:24.160
between seemingly unrelated languages&nbsp;
like English, Greek, Celtic and Sanskrit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.120
The similarities were too frequent to be&nbsp;
coincidental, and often followed similar patterns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:34.000
He theorized that several of the major language&nbsp;
groups were actually descended from the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.360
grandparent language, which linguists&nbsp;
today call Proto-Indo-European, or PIE.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.280
There are several theories about where and when&nbsp;
PIE was spoken, but the most popular puts it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:51.680
just north of the Black Sea, about 4500 to 2500&nbsp;
B.C. As these ancient people spread throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.720
Europe and Asia, their dialects diverged&nbsp;
and mixed with local populations to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:02.800
early versions of several large language groups.&nbsp;
Today, there are over 400 spoken languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.240
that descended from Proto-Indo-European,&nbsp;
comprising 3.2 billion native speakers.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:14.560
To be clear, there is no direct evidence of PIE.&nbsp;
It exists only in theory, though that theory is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:20.000
pretty widely accepted. Not a whole lot is known&nbsp;
about its speakers, but if we look at which words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.720
modern Indo-European languages have in common,&nbsp;
we can guess what kind of world they lived in…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.960
what food they ate, what terrain they inhabited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.240
what technologies they used. And by tracing&nbsp;
the pronunciations of words over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.120
linguists can even triangulate back&nbsp;
to what PIE may have sounded like.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.720
The Latin monere is most likely a derivative&nbsp;
of the Proto-Indo-European root men-, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.920
meant “to think.” There are thousands of words&nbsp;
around the world that descend from this source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:56.240
like the Russian mnit, the Sanskrit manayati, and&nbsp;
plenty in English like mental, memory, and mania.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.840
Now maybe it feels like we’ve gone too far back in&nbsp;
time to discover anything that would be relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:07.600
to us today. But I think it makes sense that&nbsp;
“monster” and “mind” are cognates. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.680
creatures that spring from our imagination,&nbsp;
inspired by fear or anger or guilt. Most popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.720
monsters of fiction are metaphors for something&nbsp;
psychological, whether it’s the sexual anxiety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:24.720
Dracula, the split personality of Dr. Jekyll, or&nbsp;
the grief of The Babadook. And when Jim Henson had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.880
to come up with a cast of muppets to help children&nbsp;
understand their emotions, he chose monsters.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:37.280
Etymology doesn’t just show us how words evolve&nbsp;
over time, but how ideas evolve. It’s almost like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.320
tracing the thought process of human civilization.&nbsp;
And though it’s taken hundreds of years for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:47.600
linguists to track this monster all the way back&nbsp;
to the shores of the Black Sea, in some ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:53.280
the connection was there the whole time. How many&nbsp;
of us as small children insisted that there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.520
a monster in our closet or under our bed, only to&nbsp;
have a parent assure us, “It’s all in your mind.”

